\label{fig:m4_vm1_workflow}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Snapshot Generation \& Disaster Recovery Verification}
To demonstrate data durability against compute failure:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Volume Detachment \& Snapshot Creation:}
    The filesystem was unmounted and detached from \texttt{module3-web-sever}. A point-in-time snapshot \texttt{module4-data-snapshot} (1 GiB) was captured (Figure~\ref{fig:m4_volume_snapshot}).
    
    \item \textbf{Volume Restoration from Snapshot:}
    A new independent volume, \nolinkurl{module4-restored-volume}, was instantiated directly from \texttt{module4-data-snapshot}.

    \item \textbf{Attachment to Independent Recovery Instance:}
    A clean recovery VM, \nolinkurl{module4-recovery-server} (Fedora-based), was provisioned on \texttt{m2-dmz-net}, and \nolinkurl{module4-restored-volume} was attached.

    \item \textbf{Data Integrity Inspection (Figure~\ref{fig:m4_recovery_verify}):}
    On the recovery VM, the restored disk was mounted directly without re-formatting:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash, basicstyle=\ttfamily\scriptsize]
[fedora@module4-recovery-server ~]$ sudo mount /dev/vdb /mnt/recovered
[fedora@module4-recovery-server ~]$ sudo cat /mnt/recovered/index.html
\end{lstlisting}
    The file \texttt{index.html} was retrieved 100\% intact, proving zero data loss across instance teardown and reconstruction.
\end{enumerate}

